The Go function reverses a string by converting it to a slice of runes (which correctly handles multi-byte UTF-8 characters), then swapping elements from the ends inward using the two-pointer technique.

```go
// Package reversestring provides a function to reverse a string.
package reversestring

// Reverse returns its argument string reversed rune-wise.
// It correctly handles multi-byte UTF-8 characters (e.g., emoji, accented chars).
func Reverse(s string) string {
	runes := []rune(s)
	for i, j := 0, len(runes)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
		runes[i], runes[j] = runes[j], runes[i]
	}
	return string(runes)
}
```

**Why this approach:**
- Go strings are immutable and stored as UTF-8 byte sequences.
- Operating on `[]byte` would break multi-byte characters (emoji, CJK, accented letters).
- Converting to `[]rune` decodes each Unicode code point correctly.
- The in-place swap (two pointers converging) is O(n) time and O(n) space.

